{"t": 1795766400.63, "stage": "critic", "msg": "Physics: All thirty keys check out numerically and the coverage is well spread across mechanics, heat, electromagnetism, optics and modern physics, with no duplicate testing (Q20/Q29 and Q5/Q24 are genuinely different skills). The level, however, leans heavily on single-formula recall (Q3, Q8, Q13, Q23, Q30 are near one-liners), while Q7 and Q25 are the only multi-statement reasoning items, so consider upgrading two or three of the trivial recalls to two-step problems. Several items (Q2, Q10, Q17, Q23) carry all their data in the figure, so the artwork must be checked against the stated keys before printing.", "level": "info"}
{"t": 1795766400.63, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[minor] Statement I merely restates the given data (\"Young's moduli are 2Y and Y\"), so it is a free giveaway; replace it with a non-trivial claim (e.g. about equal-load strains) to keep the four-statement item discriminating.", "level": "info", "qno": 7}
{"t": 1795766400.63, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[minor] The parenthetical double-ratio phrasing (\"incident-wave momentum density in the two setups\") is unidiomatic and risks being read as the total momentum density near the reflecting surface; state it plainly as \"the ratio of the momentum densities of the incident wave in the two cases\".", "level": "info", "qno": 16}
{"t": 1795766400.63, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[minor] The stem's forward reference \"for the diatomic gas mentioned below\" is clumsy and mixes two different gases in one setup; rewrite as \"Consider an ideal gas with fixed molecular diameter (assume a rigid diatomic molecule where relevant)\".", "level": "info", "qno": 25}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "Chemistry: The section is well spread across physical, inorganic and organic chemistry and almost all keys check out numerically (32, 38, 44, 49, 52, 53, 57, 60 are clean, single-concept items pitched at roughly the right 60\u201390 s level). Difficulty is slightly uneven: Q35, Q44 and Q52 are near-trivial one-step recall while Q50 is wordy and carries a chemically incorrect explanation, and Q31/Q40 duplicate the high-spin d6 magnetic-moment fact. Figure-dependent items (51, 55, 58, 59) could not be fully verified against their artwork and should be checked separately before printing.", "level": "info"}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[minor] This item re-tests the same fact already examined in Q31 (high-spin d6 has four unpaired electrons, \u03bc = 4.90 BM), so one of the two should be replaced with a different d-block concept (e.g., oxidation-state stability or lanthanoid contraction).", "level": "info", "qno": 40}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[minor] The stem's markup is broken \u2014 literal \"\\n\" sequences and stray $ delimiters wrap ordinary words (\"$smog.\\nI$\", \"$oxidising.\\nIII$\", \"$tract.\\nWhich$\") \u2014 and must be re-typeset before printing.", "level": "info", "qno": 46}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[major] The keyed option A gives the right products but a false rationale: NCl3 hydrolysis is not a redox process (N remains \u22123 and Cl +1 throughout); the accepted explanation is the reversed N\u2013Cl bond polarity/electronegativity versus P\u2013Cl, so the option text must be rewritten.", "level": "info", "qno": 50}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "English Proficiency: The section covers the expected BITSAT spread \u2014 grammar, synonyms/antonyms, idiom, para jumble and a short reading passage \u2014 and the keys are otherwise sound, with the RC passage well written and its two questions pitched at different levels (main idea vs. detail). Difficulty is uneven, though: q63, q64 and q66 are close to school-level recall, so at least one vocabulary item should be sharpened to match the rest. Only q61 needs a structural rewrite; the other fixes are cosmetic.", "level": "info"}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[major] The stem orders the candidate to identify the part containing a subject-verb agreement error while the key is 'No error', and part (d) is never offered as an option, so the item must be rewritten as 'Identify the part that contains an error, if any' with (a)-(d) plus 'No error' as choices.", "level": "info", "qno": 61}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[minor] The item is too trivial for BITSAT since 'vague', 'complex' and 'lengthy' are all obviously incompatible with 'none of the hikers took a wrong turn'; use closer distractors such as 'meticulous', 'succinct' or 'cursory' to raise the discrimination.", "level": "info", "qno": 63}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "[minor] The item is tagged 'Sentence Completion (vocabulary)' but is actually a word-analogy question, so the label (and the duplicate tag shared with q63) should be corrected to 'Analogy'.", "level": "info", "qno": 68}
{"t": 1795766431.19, "stage": "critic", "msg": "Logical Reasoning: The section spans a sensible mix of verbal, symbolic and visual reasoning, but it cannot go to print as is: Q78 and Q83 carry no artwork and test the identical odd-figure-out skill, and Q85 has two fully defensible options. Difficulty is uneven \u2014 Q73, Q87 and Q90 are 10\u201320 second recall/computation tasks while Q76 is needlessly cryptic \u2014 so replace the duplicate figure item with a moderate multi-step arrangement or blood-relation puzzle and tighten the wording fixes noted above.", "level": "info"}
